"hello" len 5 eq assert
"hello" 0 get must 104 eq assert
"ab" "cd" cat "abcd" eq assert

-- ===== Tuples =====
(10 20 30) apply 30 eq assert 20 eq assert 10 eq assert

-- ===== Records =====
{'x 10 'y 20} 'x at 10 eq assert
-- a field may hold a result, and must unwraps it
{'r 5 ok} 'r at must 5 eq assert
{'r 5 ok} (must 1 plus ok) 'r edit 'r at must 6 eq assert

-- edit takes the body first and its key last
{'x 1} (1 plus) 'x edit 'x at 2 eq assert

-- ===== Records: into =====
{'x 10 'y 20} 30 'x into 'x at 30 eq assert

-- into replaces a key in place, so field order never moves. A replacement of a
-- different width has to slide the fields after it, which is the case a
-- same-width overwrite above cannot catch: every position in
-- the record is exercised, because the first and last field are the two that a
-- wrong offset gets right by accident.
{'x 1 'y 2 'z 3} [7 8 9] 'x into {'x [7 8 9] 'y 2 'z 3} eq assert
{'x 1 'y 2 'z 3} [7 8 9] 'y into {'x 1 'y [7 8 9] 'z 3} eq assert
{'x 1 'y 2 'z 3} [7 8 9] 'z into {'x 1 'y 2 'z [7 8 9]} eq assert
{'x [1 2 3] 'y 2 'z 3} 9 'x into {'x 9 'y 2 'z 3} eq assert
{'x 1 'y [1 2 3] 'z 3} 9 'y into {'x 1 'y 9 'z 3} eq assert
{'x 1 'y 2 'z [1 2 3]} 9 'z into {'x 1 'y 2 'z 9} eq assert

-- A 20001-slot record field: built in the literal, then a wider replacement, then
-- a narrower one. 'n is read back each time; `at` walks from the header, so it only finds
-- 'n if the big field's slot count is right.
{'n 1 'big 0 20000 range}
  dup 'n at 1 eq assert
  0 30000 range 'big into
  dup 'n at 1 eq assert
  5 'big into
  dup 'big at 5 eq assert
  'n at 1 eq assert

-- ===== Box =====
42 box free
-- a body taken out of a list keeps its effect
{'b 1} [({'a 1})] first apply 'a at 1 eq assert 'b at 1 eq assert
-- each over a list push built
[] {'b 1} push (dup drop) each 0 get must 'b at 1 eq assert
-- nth reads a bound list's elements, keys and all
[{'a 1} {'a 2}] 'nl let 1 'nl nth must 'a at 2 eq assert
-- default never runs its fallback: a body there is the value
(1) ok (1 0 div) default apply 1 eq assert
-- a word that thens its input takes 'ok and 'no values
((1 plus ok) then) 'then-inc let 5 ok then-inc must 6 eq assert 5 no then-inc {'ok (drop 1) 'no (drop 2)} case 2 eq assert
-- then leaves the body's payload, keys and all
{'a 1} ok ((1 plus) 'a edit ok) then must 'a at 2 eq assert
-- a then body reads keys from the ok payload
{'a 1} ok ('a at ok) then must 1 eq assert
-- a case default that cannot run need not match the clauses' type
1 ok {'ok (drop 5) 'no (drop 6)} case 5 eq assert
42 box (21 mul) lend 882 eq assert free
42 box (1 plus) mutate () lend 43 eq assert free

